  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the first term of an arithmetic series if its $5$th term is $-0.28$ and the sum of the first $12$ terms of the series is $-12.$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=12$ and ${S}_{N}=-12$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{12}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(12-1)d) \\\\ -12 & =6(2{a}_{1}+11d) \\\\ -2 & =2{a}_{1}+11d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $5$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)d \\\\ -0.28 & ={a}_{1}+4d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-0.28={a}_{1}+4d \\\\ -2=2{a}_{1}+11d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56 & =-2{a}_{1}-8d \\\\ -2 & =2{a}_{1}+11d \\\\ -1.44 & =3d \\\\ d & =-0.48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=-0.48$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.28 & ={a}_{1}+4(-0.48) \\\\ -0.28 & ={a}_{1}-1.92 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
